  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote the procedural tree app in src/main.ts, src/tree.ts and src/meshes.ts. It passes `typecheck`, but I couldn't run it or `vite build`, so I haven't seen a rendered frame.\n\nThe layout comes from a seeded RNG (default seed 20240611). You can override it with `?seed=N`, and `?season=autumn` switches the palette to autumn colours.\n\n- **Branches:** 6 surface roots, a flared trunk, 7 major limbs, 21 branches and 42 twigs, 77 in total. They are tapered tubes merged into one vertex-coloured mesh, with ridged bark.\n- **Foliage:** about 60 lumpy leaf clumps, one instanced mesh, placed at the twig, branch and limb ends. Each has its own size, yaw and one of 6 green tones, lighter toward the top.\n- **Details:**\n  - A knot and hollow on the trunk.\n  - A rope swing under the lowest limb.\n  - A bird house hanging from the second limb.\n  - 5 mushrooms and 36 fallen leaves on the mound.\n- **Scene:** a heightfield mound with vertex-coloured grass, a procedural sky, fog and a shadow-casting key light, so the crown should cast a shadow on the mound. The camera distance is computed from the generated tree's bounds, so the whole tree is framed. It orbits slowly through `interactions.orbit` with `autoRotate`.\n- **State:** `window.__TASK_STATE__` and `window.__EVAL_READY__` are set two animation frames after the first engine frame callback. `branchCount` includes the roots.\n\nI couldn't confirm the colours, framing or shadow placement visually. The vertex colour values in particular are my guess at how the engine treats them.\n\n",
